# ComposeUX Audio [SPEC:CXC-AUDIO]

> Sound is an actuator, like a screen. "State on the bus, sound in the widget." A `red_alert` bit on the bus triggers an alarm; the config declares *what to play on what trigger*; a per-platform audio backend realizes it — I2S tone, an MP3 off a DFPlayer, a MIDI note on a SAM2695, HDMI audio on a Pi. Define-once intent, many backends — the render-engine pattern applied to audio. Works with or without a head.

---

## 1. Intent

### [CXC-AUDIO-INTENT] Sound Is an Output Peripheral, Parallel to the Display

- definition: a ComposeUX surface (headed or headless) can emit audio in response to bus state. The unit declares a **sound intent bound to a trigger** ("play `alarm` while `ship/red_alert` is on"); the platform's audio backend performs it. The config never carries audio samples — it carries the *intent*, exactly as it carries drawing intent, not pixels.
- key-insight: this is the **Canvas-contract pattern applied to audio**. Just as "own every pixel" gave a ~30-fn Canvas contract implemented per display backend, audio gives a small **Audio contract** implemented per audio backend. The widget/config is backend-agnostic; portability is by construction.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-AUDIO]

### [CXC-AUDIO-HEADLESS] Sound Decouples from the Head

- insight: audio is an output the way pixels are — so a **headless** widget can play sound. A bus-triggered headless widget sounds an alarm when `jumpdrive/readiness` flips; a **sound-only ESP32** (a DFPlayer + no screen) is a headless ComposeUX node whose *peripheral is audio instead of pixels*. This is [CXC-HW-INTENT] taken at its word — "the silicon's job shrinks to map signals ↔ peripherals," and a speaker is just another peripheral.
- consequence: the audio capability lives at the widget/runtime layer, not the render layer, so it is available on the headless runtime and on visual surfaces alike.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-AUDIO]

---

## 2. The Model

### [CXC-AUDIO-CONTRACT] The Audio Contract (intent set — to be bounded)

- definition: a small, closed set of audio intents a backend implements — the audio analog of the Canvas contract. Candidate set (to ratify):
  - `play_clip(id)` — play a named clip/file (a DFPlayer track, an SD WAV/MP3, a bundled asset).
  - `play_note(note, dur)` / `play_tone(hz, dur)` — a parametric note/tone (MIDI note on a synth; a synthesized I2S tone).
  - `stop()` · `set_volume(gain)` · `loop(on)` — transport/level control.
- discipline: like the DSL and the Canvas contract, the set is CLOSED and small so it's realizable on a $3 MCU and a Pi alike; growth is a spec change, not an author liberty.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-AUDIO]

### [CXC-AUDIO-BACKENDS] One Intent, Many Backends

- the backends (each realizes the contract for its hardware):
  - **I2S / DAC** (ESP32, MinnowBoard) — synthesized tones or streamed WAV to an I2S DAC/amp (MAX98357A-class).
  - **DFPlayer Mini** (ESP32, any UART host) — serial "play track N" of an MP3 on the module's SD card. `play_clip(id)` → track number; highest-level, cheapest.
  - **MIDI synth** — SAM2695 (Seeed XIAO MIDI Synthesizer) or similar; serial MIDI. `play_note` → MIDI note-on/off. Musical/expressive.
  - **HDMI / ALSA / CoreAudio** (RPi, MinnowBoard, desktop) — OS audio; clips + tones via the platform mixer.
- board-profile parallel: which audio backend a device has is a **board-profile** fact ([CXC-RM-M5] `board_profile.h`), just like its display driver — a device declares its audio backend + capabilities; the same widget config plays on whatever is wired.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-AUDIO]

### [CXC-AUDIO-BIND] Declarative Trigger → Sound

- model: a sound is bound to a trigger the same way a behavior is ([CXC-BIND]) — `when ship/red_alert is on → loop play_clip('alarm')`; release stops it. Bus-native and Intelligence-drivable: an Intelligence "sounds the alarm" by publishing one bus bit, identical to any other actor. Determinism where it matters (a bus-triggered headless sound fires on the observed publish, [CXC-HW-SCHEDULE]).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-AUDIO]

---

## 3. Open Decisions (before build — David to shape)

### [CXC-AUDIO-OPEN] What Needs Deciding

- **intent-set boundary**: is `play_note`/MIDI in v1, or is v1 just `play_clip` + stop/volume (the DFPlayer/alarm case) with the synth/MIDI as a follow-on?
- **clip-asset addressing**: how is a clip named + resolved? A `bus://assets/audio/<id>` asset (like display assets), a DFPlayer track-number map declared in the board profile, or both (the same "fungible device" pattern — the config names `alarm`, the profile maps it to track 3 / a WAV path)?
- **widget shape**: is audio a **behavior** on any widget (a `play:` behavior alongside blink/pulse), a dedicated **actuator widget/node type**, or both? (Leaning: a behavior for headed widgets + a first-class node in Flow for headless sound units.)
- **latency/sync**: any guarantees, or best-effort per backend? (Alarms tolerate latency; anything musical/synced does not.)
- **MinnowBoard/RPi HDMI**: mixer/routing assumptions; concurrency (can two sounds overlap?).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-AUDIO]

